\lispPackageChapter{sigma/string}

The \texttt{sigma/string} package contains useful tools for working with
strings: ranges of characters, joining and repeating, trimming whitespace, and
converting other objects to text.

\section{Constants}

\lispParameterSubsection{+whitespace+}

The \texttt{+whitespace+} constant is a list of characters treated as
whitespace by the trimming functions:
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Space},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Newline},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Backspace},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Tab},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Linefeed},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Page},
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Return}, and
\texttt{\#\textbackslash Rubout}.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{character-range}

The \texttt{character-range} function returns a list of characters from the
\lispVar{start} to the \lispVar{end} character.  Note that this returns a
list, not a string.  The endpoints may be given in either order; the result is
always ascending.

\lispSyntax

\lispExample
  {(character-range \lispVar{start} \lispVar{end})}
  {'(\lispVar{start} $\ldots$ \lispVar{end})}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
  \item[\lispVar{start}] The character to start the range with, inclusive.
  \item[\lispVar{end}] The character to end the range with, inclusive.
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\lispExample
  {(character-range \#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash e)}
  {'(\#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash b \#\textbackslash c \#\textbackslash d
    \#\textbackslash e)}

\lispExample
  {(character-range \#\textbackslash e \#\textbackslash a)}
  {'(\#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash b \#\textbackslash c \#\textbackslash d
    \#\textbackslash e)}

\lispFunctionSubsection{character-ranges}

The \texttt{character-ranges} function is a convenience wrapper for
\texttt{character-range}, concatenating several calls and making the
resultant list contain only unique instances, sorted in ascending order.

\lispSyntax

\lispExample
  {(character-ranges \lispVar{start${}_1$} \lispVar{end${}_1$} $\ldots$}
  {'(\lispVar{character${}_1$} $\ldots$)}

\lispArgs

\begin{description}
  \item[\lispVar{start${}_n$}] The character to start the $n$th range with, inclusive.
  \item[\lispVar{end${}_n$}] The character to end the $n$th range with, inclusive.
\end{description}

\lispExamples

\lispExample
  {(character-ranges \#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash c
                     \#\textbackslash x \#\textbackslash z)}
  {'(\#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash b \#\textbackslash c
     \#\textbackslash x \#\textbackslash y \#\textbackslash z)}

\lispExample
  {(character-ranges \#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash c
                     \#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash c)}
  {'(\#\textbackslash a \#\textbackslash b \#\textbackslash c)}

\lispFunctionSubsection{escape-tildes}

The \texttt{escape-tildes} function returns a copy of a string in which every
tilde (\texttt{\#\textbackslash\textasciitilde}) is doubled, suitable for use as
a \texttt{format} control string that should print tildes literally.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(escape-tildes \lispVar{string})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(escape-tildes "foo bar")  ; Returns "foo bar"
(escape-tildes "foo~bar")  ; Returns "foo~~bar"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{replace-char}

The \texttt{replace-char} function replaces every instance of
\texttt{from-char} in \texttt{string} with \texttt{to-char}.  The string is
modified in place and returned.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(replace-char \lispVar{string} \lispVar{from-char} \lispVar{to-char})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(replace-char (copy-seq "banana") #\textbackslash a #\textbackslash o)
;; Returns "bonono"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{strcat}

The \texttt{strcat} function takes any number of arguments, converts each to a
string with \texttt{to-string}, and concatenates the results.  The symbol
\texttt{string-concatenate} is an alias for this function.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(strcat \&rest \lispVar{rest})}

\texttt{(string-concatenate \&rest \lispVar{rest})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(strcat "foo" "bar")           ; Returns "foobar"
(strcat "foo" 123 "bar")       ; Returns "foo123bar"
(strcat)                       ; Returns ""
(strcat 1 2 3 4)               ; Returns "1234"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{string-concatenate}

The \texttt{string-concatenate} function is an alias for \texttt{strcat}.

\lispFunctionSubsection{strmult}

The \texttt{strmult} function concatenates its string arguments (via
\texttt{strcat}) and repeats that result \texttt{count} times, returning one
combined string.  When \texttt{count} is less than $1$, it returns the empty
string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(strmult \lispVar{count} \&rest \lispVar{strings})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(strmult 3 "ab")     ; Returns "ababab"
(strmult 2 "x" "y")  ; Returns "xyxy"
(strmult 0 "nope")   ; Returns ""
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{string-join}

The \texttt{string-join} function joins a list of strings into one string,
inserting \texttt{connecting-string} between each adjacent pair.  A single
string may be passed and is returned unchanged aside from the single-element
join path.  The connecting string defaults to the empty string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(string-join \lispVar{strings} \&optional \lispVar{connecting-string})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(string-join '("a" "b" "c") "-") ; Returns "a-b-c"
(string-join '("a" "b" "c"))     ; Returns "abc"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{stringify}

The \texttt{stringify} function takes an argument of any type and converts it
to a string using the \texttt{\textasciitilde A} directive to \texttt{format}
inside \texttt{with-standard-io-syntax}, so the result does not depend on the
caller's printer controls (for example \texttt{*print-case*} or
\texttt{*print-base*}).  Also see \texttt{to-string}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(stringify \lispVar{argument})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(stringify 12)   ; Returns "12"
(stringify :foo) ; Returns "FOO"
(let ((*print-case* :downcase) (*print-base* 16))
  (stringify :foo)  ; Still "FOO"
  (stringify 255))  ; Still "255"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{string-trim-whitespace}

The \texttt{string-trim-whitespace} function removes whitespace from both ends
of a string, using the characters in \texttt{+whitespace+}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(string-trim-whitespace \lispVar{string})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(string-trim-whitespace "   foo   ") ; Returns "foo"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{string-left-trim-whitespace}

The \texttt{string-left-trim-whitespace} function removes whitespace from the
left side only of a string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(string-left-trim-whitespace \lispVar{string})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(string-left-trim-whitespace "   foo   ") ; Returns "foo   "
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{string-right-trim-whitespace}

The \texttt{string-right-trim-whitespace} function removes whitespace from the
right side only of a string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(string-right-trim-whitespace \lispVar{string})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(string-right-trim-whitespace "   foo   ") ; Returns "   foo"
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{to-string}

The \texttt{to-string} function converts common types of things into a string.
It handles some special cases more usefully than \texttt{stringify} for most
user-facing output: \texttt{nil} becomes the empty string, symbols become
their downcased \texttt{symbol-name}s, and strings are returned unchanged.
Other values are printed with \texttt{\textasciitilde A} under
\texttt{with-standard-io-syntax}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(to-string \lispVar{s})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(to-string nil)    ; Returns ""
(to-string :foo)   ; Returns "foo"
(to-string "hello"); Returns "hello"
(to-string 255)    ; Returns "255" even if *print-base* is 16
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Methods}

\lispMethodsSubsection{split}

The \texttt{split} method on strings splits a string into a list of substrings
on \texttt{separators}.  Separators may be a single separator or a sequence of
them.  The \texttt{key} and \texttt{test} arguments control membership tests,
and \texttt{remove-separators?} controls whether separator text is kept.  The
implementation coerces the string to a list, delegates to the list method of
\texttt{split} from \texttt{sigma/sequence}, and coerces each piece back to a
string.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(split \lispVar{string} \lispVar{separators} \&key \lispVar{key}
  \lispVar{test} \lispVar{remove-separators?})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(split "a,b,c" #\,) ; Returns '("a" "b" "c")
\end{lstlisting}
